 fachadas en abril, y su alcalde inauguró la plaza con una banda de música.","passed":false,"expected_active":true,"activation":0.599246,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Cross-lingual positive holdout with Spanish su and sus binding following nouns to identifiable possessors, predicted active by the hypothesis.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"After her diagnosis, the patient's family asked that her records be transferred to the clinic's archive.","passed":false,"expected_active":true,"activation":0.505924,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Novel English context in which her and the 's clitic each bind a following noun to a specific possessor, so the hypothesis predicts activation.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"The Lessee shall return the Landlord's keys and remove their belongings from the premises before the Termination Date.","passed":false,"expected_active":true,"activation":0.307715,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Positive holdout from a legal-contract genre absent from the scan corpus, with a genitive clitic and their binding nouns, which the hypothesis predicts should fire.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"Maya locked the studio and checked her phone.\nHer brother's van was still parked outside the gallery.","passed":true,"expected_active":true,"activation":0.973507,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Positive holdout with the possessive phrase in an unusual position, opening a line after a newline with her and brother's binding nouns, which the hypothesis says should still activate.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"On the first Monday of April, the restoration crew arrived at the old railway depot with crates of tools and a van full of scaffolding. The building had stood empty for eleven years. Weeds pushed through the loading platform, and pigeons nested in the roof lantern. The council had voted to turn the depot into a community archive, but the budget covered only the shell of the building and none of the interior work. Maria Ortega, who had spent two decades cataloguing municipal records, was appointed to lead the project. She walked the length of the depot each morning before the others arrived, noting where the brickwork had crumbled and where the iron columns needed paint. The first month passed in dust and noise. Volunteers cleared the offices, removed rotten floorboards and stacked the salvaged timber in the yard. By June the roof was watertight and the electricians had begun to run cable through the old service ducts. The work was slower than anyone had promised. A survey in July found asbestos in the ceiling panels, and the crew lost three weeks while a licensed contractor sealed the affected rooms. Donations paid for two of the display cases, and a local printer supplied the labels at cost. In August the archivists began to move the first boxes out of the damp basement of the town hall, where they had waited through two floods and a decade of neglect. Each box was opened on a trestle table, dusted with a soft brush and checked against a list typed on a machine that no longer worked. The opening was set for the last Saturday in September. On the Friday before, a delivery of shelving failed to arrive, and the volunteers spent the evening building temporary racks out of pallets and borrowed trestles. Maria's desk stood where the stationmaster's office had once been, and her name was painted on the door in small gold letters. The mayor cut the ribbon at eleven o'clock, and the first visitors walked past the rows of ledgers to the reading room at the far end of the hall. In the weeks that followed, the archive received a steady trickle of donations: a box of railway timetables, a ledger from a grocer's co-operative, and a set of photographs taken during the construction of the viaduct. Two school classes visited before Christmas, and the children were allowed to hold the brass ticket punch that had once sat on the counter. The council renewed the project's funding for a further year, though the grant was smaller than before and the volunteers were told to expect fewer staff from the town hall. Maria began to plan an oral history programme, recording the memories of former railway workers before the last of them moved away.","passed":false,"expected_active":true,"activation":0.481274,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Long-context positive holdout of about 550 tokens in which the possessive phrases (Maria's, stationmaster's, her, grocer's, project's) sit deep inside the passage, past any short context window, and the hypothesis predicts they should still activate.","measured":true},{"kind":"hard_negative","prompt":"The nurse reassured her before the surgery and handed her a glass of water.","passed":true,"expected_active":false,"activation":0.201609,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Hard negative: the same string ' her' appears twice as an object pronoun binding no following noun, the exact case the hypothesis says stays low.","measured":true},{"kind":"hard_negative","prompt":"It's already nine o'clock, and he's still waiting for the bus that's running late.","passed":false,"expected_active":false,"activation":0.708012,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Hard negative: three occurrences of the 's token as a contraction of is rather than a genitive clitic, so the hypothesis predicts no activation.","measured":true},{"kind":"hard_negative","prompt":"That leather jacket in the hall is his, though he never wears it anymore.","passed":false,"expected_active":false,"activation":0.804255,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Hard negative: ' his' is a predicative possessive that binds no following noun, and the hypothesis's trigger requires a determiner binding a noun, so it predicts low.","measured":true},{"kind":"hard_negative","prompt":"